#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493956 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{x}{4}\\le y\\le 2x,\\,1\\le xy\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{2y}{x}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{-v/2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1/4}^{2}]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{4}]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot {[-2{e}^{-v/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot ({e}^{-2}-{e}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493973 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+3y$ and $v=x+2y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x+2y\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=x+2y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |-1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495059 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y+2x.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|-\\frac{1}{4}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|]_{2}^{6}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495084 :question/problem "![](images/d4/d459697b7a62f75b89093ab5d12ec9b2087f4d4854cbb91ad7fffab8d13a02d1.png)\n\nFind the slope of the line shown above." :question/worked-solution "The slope of the vertical line is undefined. We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(-1,0)$ and $(-1,1),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{1-0}{-1-(-1)}=\\frac{1-0}{-1+1}=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495100 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=xy\\,\\text{and}\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2y}{x}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{2v}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{1}{2v}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)[{\\int }_{1}^{5}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u){[\\frac{1}{2}\\,{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{5}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\cdot 12\\,du \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\,du \\\\  & =6{[-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi u)]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495119 :question/problem "![](images/93/939cada46a127b1b8ea90a3f2daf643974b1ba31a8f6c88230d8d4ca0e43e5d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the slope of the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that slope is the amount the line rises vertically per unit that it runs horizontally.\n\nSince the graph increases ($↗$) as we go from left to right, the line rises by a positive amount.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495128 :question/problem "![](images/76/76cd6a4f07d424a3ce8ae6d2f939e52f5aa2fc66331f671c4b926b1ee5fdae8c.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the slope of the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that slope is the amount the line rises vertically per unit that it runs horizontally.\n\nSince the graph decreases ($↘$) as we go from left to right, the line rises by a negative amount.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495150 :question/problem "![](images/27/27763c2083cbfc9961a619fb8e4f9bbb50791f446b734aef2a4f822e5d04de4b.png)\n\nDetermine the slope of the line shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line is horizontal, and therefore it has a slope of zero.\n\n We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(0,0)$ and $(3,0),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{0-0}{3-0}=\\frac{0}{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495158 :question/problem "![](images/20/2026a71a7cb647e7a5d844e9c5259f73321d7fd8097255245dc99f87dfc60a92.png)\n\nThe straight line shown above intersects the $y$-axis when $y=5$ and intersects the $x$-axis when $x=7.$ Find its slope." :question/worked-solution "Note that the line passes through the points $(0,5)$ and $(7,0).$ So, we let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,0),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-5}{7-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $-\\frac{5}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496226 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4u-6v &=-3 \\\\ u &=-v-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(u,v)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(u,v)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the second equation is already solved for $u:$\n\n$$\nu=-v-2\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $u$ with $-v-2$ in the first equation and solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-6v & =-3 \\\\ 4(-v-2)-6v & =-3 \\\\ -4v-8-6v & =-3 \\\\ -10v & =5 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into $u=-v-2,$ then we can find the value of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-v-2 \\\\ u & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-2 \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496252 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $y-x?$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}21x-9y &=0 \\\\ 5y-15x+10 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}21x-9y &=0 && \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 5y-15x+10 &=0 && \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(2),$ we can isolate $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-15x+10 & =0 \\\\ 5y & =15x-10 \\\\ y & =3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $3x-2$  into equation $(1)$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21x-9y & =0 \\\\ 21x-9(3x-2) & =0 \\\\ 21x-27x+18 & =0 \\\\ -6x+18 & =0 \\\\ 18 & =6x \\\\ 3 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=3$ into $y=3x-2,$ then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-2 \\\\ y & =3(3)-2 \\\\ y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3,\\,y=7.$ So,\n\n$$\ny-x=7-3=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496255 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y &=-4 \\\\ x-3y &=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(x,y)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the second equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $3y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\nx=3y-8\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $x$ with $3y-8$ in the first equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-4 \\\\ -2(3y-8)+y & =-4 \\\\ -6y+16+y & =-4 \\\\ -5y & =-20 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into $x=3y-8,$ then we can find the value of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3y-8 \\\\ x & =3(4)-8 \\\\ x & =12-8 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=4,\\,y=4,$ which can be expressed as $(4,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496314 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p-6q &=-18 \\\\ 4p+3q+2 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(p,q)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(p,q)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p-6q &=-18 && \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 4p+3q+2 &=0 && \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(1),$ we can solve for $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-6q & =-18 \\\\ 3p & =6q-18 \\\\ p & =2q-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=2q-6$ into equation $(2)$ and solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2q-6)+3q+2 & =0 \\\\ 8q-24+3q+2 & =0 \\\\ 11q & =22 \\\\ q & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $q=2$ into equation $(1),$ then we can find the value of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-6(2) & =-18 \\\\ 3p-12 & =-18 \\\\ 3p & =-6 \\\\ p & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-2,\\,q=2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497401 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz$ at the point $(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+yz)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xz)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(0)(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(1))\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(0))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(1,0,1)=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497411 :question/problem "The pastry shop's total cost and revenue per month (in dollars) from selling cakes can be modeled by the equations $y=1\\,600+5n$ and $y=64+17n,$ respectively, where $n$ is the number of cakes sold. \n\nSolve the system below using the substitution method to find the exact number of cakes needed to break even and the revenue made.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1\\,600+5n \\\\ y &=64+17n\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe pastry shop breaks even when$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes are produced. \n\nThe revenue made when selling this number of cakes is$\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that $y$ is isolated in the first equation. Therefore, we can replace $y$ with $1\\,600+5n$ in the second equation and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n-64 & =64+17n-64 \\\\ 1\\,536+5n & =17n \\\\ 1\\,536+5n-5n & =17n-5n \\\\ 1\\,536 & =12n \\\\ \\frac{1\\,536}{12} & =\\frac{12n}{12} \\\\ 128 & =n \\\\ n & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $n=128$ into $y=64+17n$, then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=64+17(128)=2\\,240\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $n=128$ and $y=2\\,240.$\nTherefore, the pastry shop breaks even when $128$ units are produced, and the revenue made when selling this number of units is $2\\,240$ dollars."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497648 :question/problem "Let $g=\\begin{bmatrix}{r}^{2} \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $h=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ {r}^{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r>0$, find the value of $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot {r}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}-2{r}^{2}-6 \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =3 \\\\ r & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $r>0.$ Therefore, $r=\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497657 :question/problem "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $a<0$, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $u⊥v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {v}_{z} \\\\  & =a\\cdot a+2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ {a}^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ {a}^{2} & =10 \\\\ a & =\\pm \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $a<0.$ Therefore, $a=-\\sqrt{10}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497669 :question/problem "If $c=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 3k \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},$ $d=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and $c⊥d,$ then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $c⊥d$ ($c$ and $d$ are perpendicular) if and only if $c\\cdot d=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-3)+3k\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3k+6k-8 \\\\  & =3k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =0 \\\\ 3k-8 & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497671 :question/problem "Let $g=\\begin{bmatrix}4{r}^{2} \\\\ -2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $h=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r<0$, find the value of $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 2{r}^{2}+(-10)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =8{r}^{2}-4{r}^{2}-30 \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2}-30 & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2} & =30 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $r<0.$ Therefore, $r=-\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497673 :question/problem "Let $g=\\begin{bmatrix}3{r}^{2} \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $h=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r<0$, find the value of $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 2{r}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9{r}^{2}-4{r}^{2}-24 \\\\  & =5{r}^{2}-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 5{r}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 5{r}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{24}{5} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{30}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $r<0.$ Therefore, $r=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{30}}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497687 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨{x}^{2},-1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩.$ If $a⊥b$ and $x<0,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $a⊥b$ ($a$ and $b$ are perpendicular) if and only if $a\\cdot b=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $x<0.$ Therefore, $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497690 :question/problem "Let $g=\\begin{bmatrix}4{r}^{2} \\\\ -4 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$ and $h=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r<0$, find the value of $r.$\n\n$r=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 3{r}^{2}+(-9)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16{r}^{2}-12{r}^{2}-18 \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $r<0.$ Therefore, $r=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497694 :question/problem "Let $g=\\begin{bmatrix}3{r}^{2} \\\\ -4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $h=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r>0$, find the value of $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 2{r}^{2}+(-6)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12{r}^{2}-8{r}^{2}-18 \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $r>0.$ Therefore, $r=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497695 :question/problem "Let $g=\\begin{bmatrix}4{r}^{2} \\\\ -3 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $h=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r>0$, find the value of $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 2{r}^{2}+(-10)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12{r}^{2}-6{r}^{2}-20 \\\\  & =6{r}^{2}-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 6{r}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 6{r}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $r>0.$ Therefore, $r=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497705 :question/problem "If $a=⟨3.9,-2.5,4.3⟩$ and $b=⟨-5,-3,-3.7⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =3.9\\cdot (-5)+(-2.5)\\cdot (-3)+4.3\\cdot (-3.7) \\\\  & =-19.5+7.5-15.91 \\\\  & =-27.91.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497712 :question/problem "If $a=⟨3.8,3.6,-1.1⟩$ and $b=⟨3.2,-3.4,-2⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =3.8\\cdot 3.2+3.6\\cdot (-3.4)+(-1.1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =12.16-12.24+2.2 \\\\  & =2.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497721 :question/problem "If $a=⟨-9,11,-4⟩$ and $b=⟨7,-3,-4⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =(-9)\\cdot 7+11\\cdot (-3)+(-4)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-63-33+16 \\\\  & =-80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497722 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-7,-9,6⟩$ and $b=⟨6,12,8⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot b.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot 6+(-9)\\cdot 12+6\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-42-108+48 \\\\  & =-102\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497726 :question/problem "If $a=⟨3,-3,12⟩$ and $b=⟨9,-10,-9⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9+(-3)\\cdot (-10)+12\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =27+30-108 \\\\  & =-51.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497731 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨3,-3.8,-2.8⟩$ and $b=⟨2.8,-5.2,3.6⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot b.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2.8+(-3.8)\\cdot (-5.2)+(-2.8)\\cdot 3.6 \\\\  & =8.4+19.76-10.08 \\\\  & =18.08\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497732 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨4.9,5.7,3.8⟩$ and $b=⟨1,5.4,-4.7⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot b.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4.9\\cdot 1+5.7\\cdot 5.4+3.8\\cdot (-4.7) \\\\  & =4.9+30.78-17.86 \\\\  & =17.82\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497738 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨8,4,10⟩$ and $b=⟨3,7,-8⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot b.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 7+10\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =24+28-80 \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497739 :question/problem "If $a=⟨4,11,4⟩$ and $b=⟨8,-11,3⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8+11\\cdot (-11)+4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =32-121+12 \\\\  & =-77.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497741 :question/problem "If $a=⟨3.9,4.9,-5.9⟩$ and $b=⟨-5.6,1.8,3.2⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =3.9\\cdot (-5.6)+4.9\\cdot 1.8+(-5.9)\\cdot 3.2 \\\\  & =-21.84+8.82-18.88 \\\\  & =-31.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497745 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-4x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4x \\\\  & =-4(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4x \\\\  & =-4(1) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-4).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-4x.$ \n\n![](images/8b/8b008b94eebbd24ae51670447848ce2d644c067d43dd92d72de85b048358cc54.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497762 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-3x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\  & =-3(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\  & =-3(1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-3x.$ \n\n![](images/08/08e854b04d47ff670b197a2a0c7b51c13eb135626e0ed6cdfd24368d4032f259.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497764 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-1x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1x \\\\  & =-1(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1x \\\\  & =-1(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-1x.$ \n\n![](images/73/7322801cf231391f58d39f2fad42b779e04fc1b81b686bcb3291ebfee5651226.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497774 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-x-3.$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-x-3.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=-(0)-3=-3,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-(1)-3=-4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=-(2)-3=-5,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-(3)-3=-6.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $-6$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c9/c96b7da7b073986de68a557df8a9dbde16d39566c87eb0b5ba6ac62bd40da7c4.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-x-3.$\n\n![](images/05/05d10ee3d2e6bc295a7d2e6b07ca726c9c11c159d74f1d81c71fce761fa4275e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497775 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x-6.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x-6.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2(0)-6=-6,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)-6=-4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)-6=-2,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)-6=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/95/958c5a848a09e43832324b8afb000348c7630cd2c42e99a51215d48d9895a00e.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of  $y=2x-6.$\n\n![](images/3e/3efb14f33400cd4a6353b93e5d99a826083cca6859785d5bd5d837fb00ad302f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497777 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=x-6.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=x-6.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=(0)-6=-6,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=(1)-6=-5,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=(2)-6=-4,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=(3)-6=-3.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-3$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/99/99188f2a0f8de0054a6375a88a11242bea7e4799cc17552cd9ba0dd00d5a0788.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=x-6.$\n\n![](images/44/44b8f095ef9aca54e6215b1b61c9fad562dd54b0f00b7e9b8e2efdd904dd83e6.png)"}]}
